<p class="small">The Prospecting Show · Editorial sales resource · Published October 12, 2026 · Updated November 22, 2026</p>
<p>A manufacturing-software ideal customer profile should describe a production decision your software can support, the people who own it, and the data needed to make it useful. “Manufacturers with more than a certain number of employees” is incomplete unless that filter follows from your actual delivery requirements.</p>
<p>This guide is for vendors selling software to manufacturers. The fictional offer below is a read-only production-exception review using an approved sample export. It is not a machine-control system, a production deployment, or a promise to improve plant performance. Every account, job, and number in the exercise is fictional.</p>
<nav aria-label="Guide contents"><ul><li><a href="#offer">Define the supported decision</a></li><li><a href="#gates">Check workflow and delivery fit</a></li><li><a href="#sample">Test the data with four jobs</a></li><li><a href="#accounts">Compare account decisions</a></li><li><a href="#records">Download the practice records</a></li></ul></nav>
<h2 id="offer">Define one decision before choosing accounts</h2>
<p>Suppose the offer helps a production supervisor review jobs whose observed start differs from the plan. The input is a buyer-approved, redacted sample containing stable job identifiers, planned starts, actual starts where known, and an explicit status observed at a particular time. The output is a reviewed exception register and a list of unresolved data definitions.</p>
<p>The provider can compare a supported export and present a read-only review. The buyer owns the interpretation and any production action. The offer excludes writing to equipment, changing schedules automatically, guaranteeing uptime, or replacing an unreviewed operational control. Record those boundaries before a rep calls an account qualified.</p>
<h2 id="gates">Use evidence gates that another rep can apply</h2>
<div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Manufacturing software account fit gates" tabindex="0"><table><caption>Fit conditions for this fictional exception-review offer</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Gate</th><th scope="col">Evidence to confirm</th><th scope="col">Finding that pauses or ends this sales path</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">A recurring decision exists</th><td>The supervisor describes who reviews start exceptions, when, and what decision follows.</td><td>A seller assumes that every factory has the same reporting problem.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">The workflow fits the offer</th><td>A read-only review can support the buyer's defined question.</td><td>The buyer requires direct machine control or another capability outside this offer.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Definitions can be agreed</th><td>The buyer can distinguish planned start, observed actual start, confirmed not started, and unknown status.</td><td>A blank timestamp is silently treated as proof the job has not started.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Inputs can be used appropriately</th><td>The data owner approves a redacted sample and the agreed handling process.</td><td>No authorized sample can be provided, or required identifiers and events cannot be resolved.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Delivery has owners</th><td>Production and IT roles can review the sample, confirm limits, and own remaining dependencies.</td><td>An unsupported integration or unavailable internal reviewer is necessary to demonstrate the proposed task.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Mark each gate confirmed, contradicted, or unknown. Do not average away a contradicted capability requirement with a favorable company size. If your actual offer supports different production processes or input formats, use those documented capabilities to define the profile rather than copying this exercise unchanged.</p>
<h2 id="sample">A four-job sample makes a hidden assumption visible</h2>
<p>All times below refer to one fictional day, in the same local clock, with an observation at 10:00. The exercise does not cross midnight or convert time zones. For started jobs, actual-start deviation is actual start minus planned start. A positive value means after the plan; a negative value means before it. This is an exercise definition, not an industry tolerance standard.</p>
<div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Fictional manufacturing job review" tabindex="0"><table><caption>Missing actual-start data does not have a single meaning</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Job</th><th scope="col">Plan / actual</th><th scope="col">Status at 10:00</th><th scope="col">Supported finding</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Practice Job A</th><td>08:00 / 08:10</td><td>Started; actual start observed.</td><td>Actual-start deviation +10 minutes.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Practice Job B</th><td>08:30 / no actual start</td><td>Confirmed not started.</td><td>90 minutes past the planned start at observation. Final actual-start deviation is unknown.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Practice Job C</th><td>09:00 / 08:55</td><td>Started; actual start observed.</td><td>Actual-start deviation −5 minutes.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Practice Job D</th><td>09:30 / no actual start</td><td>Unknown; no confirmed start status.</td><td>Investigate the missing status. Do not report a 30-minute delay as an observed fact.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The rows reconcile to two observed starts, one confirmed not-started job, and one unknown: 2 + 1 + 1 = 4. Of the two jobs with observed actual starts, one began after its plan, so the share is 1/2, or 50%. That is not the late-start rate for all four jobs; B has no final start and D has no confirmed status.</p>
<p>Do not add A's 10-minute completed start deviation to B's 90-minute open overdue interval and call the result a common performance measure. They describe different observations. Do not replace D's missing value with zero. Before a pilot, ask the buyer whether these distinctions match how their team records and reviews work.</p>
<h2>Use the sample to test fit, not to imply a result</h2>
<p>A useful fit conversation can reveal that the buyer's export records only an end-of-shift status, while the proposed task needs a start event. That is an unresolved input requirement. It is more useful to document it than to show a polished dashboard with invented timestamps.</p>
<p>Ask who can approve the event definitions, how job identifiers remain consistent between exports, which missing values can be resolved, and who owns the next review. Demonstrate with fictional or appropriately approved sample data. Do not request live access merely to establish whether the offer is relevant.</p>
<h2 id="accounts">Three fictional accounts produce different decisions</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Practice Manufacturer A:</strong> a supervisor confirms a start-exception review problem. A data owner can approve a sample, distinguish unknown from not started, and join a scoped fit discussion. Advance to that discussion; no production pilot or purchase is assumed.</li><li><strong>Practice Manufacturer B:</strong> the buyer requires equipment-control capability the defined offer does not provide. Record the mismatch and stop this sales path. Urgency does not change the provider's capabilities.</li><li><strong>Practice Manufacturer C:</strong> a public expansion announcement is the only observation. No buyer has confirmed a reporting problem, sample availability, or owner. Keep it in research and ask whether the specific workflow is relevant.</li></ol>
<p>These are service-fit decisions for an offer, not statements about a manufacturer's quality or financial health. Employee count, industry category, and expansion news may help organize research, but they cannot substitute for a confirmed workflow and a workable delivery path.</p>
<h2 id="records">Download the evidence records</h2>
<p><a href="/downloads/manufacturing-start-review-fictional.csv" download>Download the four fictional job-review rows (CSV)</a> or <a href="/downloads/manufacturing-software-icp-blank.csv" download>download the blank manufacturing-software account profile (CSV)</a>. The job file preserves blank unknown values and separates actual-start deviation from open overdue time. The account-profile file contains headers only and no real company data.</p>
<p>For a real review, record the offer version, buyer-confirmed decision, production reviewer, data owner, supported input, agreed event definitions, unresolved dependencies, and next decision. Preserve the source reference and review date. Do not invent a budget, a plant condition, or a pilot deadline to complete the sheet.</p>
